  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nA prolonged heatwave remains on track for the entire week with a 594 \ndm ridge now in control. Southerly return flow will continue to \nadvect in higher dewpoints, ranging from the upper 60s to lower 70s. \nWith 850 mb temps leveling off around 19-22C, maxTs are less likely \nto overachieve with a deep dry layer no longer present in model\nsoundings. After blending NBM temps with CONS model guidance\nthrough Wednesday to nudge closer to the ECMWF MOS, daily highs\nrange from the mid 90s to near 100 degrees. The dry heat \ntranslates to daily heat index values ranging from 100-106; \nhowever, today will be closer to 100. Due to low confidence in \nseeing 4 consecutive days of heat index values at or above 105 \nat any given location, collaborated with WFO LSX/SGF to replace\nthe Extreme Heat Watch with a Heat Advisory that now runs from \nMonday through Friday. There is still a possibility a targeted \nwarning may be needed later this week, but for now this looks to\nonly occur in a few locations at best where highs near 100 \ndegrees are progged. NBM may also be overdoing nighttime lows \nthat are being factored into the heat risk, with extreme levels\ncurrently being progged for mid to late week. The heat risk also\ndoes not take into consideration the recent heatwaves we have \nhad this summer with much higher dewpoints.\n\nBy Friday, consensus has increased on model guidance for the \nridge to breakdown faster, allowing for a mid-level shortwave to\nmove downstream towards the FA. While the GFS is slower in \nbringing a sfc frontal boundary south that stalls in the \nvicinity on Saturday, the ECMWF/CMC are more progressive with \nisolated to scattered shower and thunderstorm chances on Friday \nthat is not currently being modeled well by the NBM. As a \nresult, only 20% PoPs are progged for each day. Given the \nlatest trends and increasing ensemble support, will message a \n30-40% chance of rain that may also help to bring some limited \nrelief from the heatwave. Locally up to a quarter of an inch of \nrainfall will be possible with any storms that develop. Highs \nover next weekend trend into the lower 90s with heat index \nvalues falling below advisory criteria.",
